@@ -69,6 +69,24 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a string containing JSON. The output is valid JSON, indented with four spaces by default (use the ``indent`` argument to change this), and is syntax highlighted unless ``highlight=False`` is passed::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+A JSON renderable can also be passed to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`. Long lines are not wrapped when printed this way.
+
+To print JSON without a console object, import ``print_json`` from the package::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+JSON can also be pretty printed from the command line by running the json module with the path to a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json FILE
+
